.TH KILL 1
.SH NAME
kill \- end or note a process
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kill
[
.B \-n
.I note
]
.I pid ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Kill
ends each named
.IR pid ,
by writing
.B kill
to its
.BI /proc/ pid /ctl
(see
.IR proc (3)).
The process ends at its next boundary whether or not it has a note
handler, with
.B "sys: killed"
as the word its parent's
.I wait
collects.
.PP
.B \-n
writes
.I note
to
.BI /proc/ pid /note
instead, which a process with a handler (see
.IR notify (2))
may catch and survive.
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/kill/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR ps (1),
.IR ns (1),
.IR notify (2),
.IR proc (3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The exit status is empty when every
.I pid
was reached.
It is
.B "no such process"
when
.BI /proc/ pid /ctl
or
.BI /proc/ pid /note
could not be opened, and
.B "can't kill"
when the write itself failed;
.I kill
still tries every
.I pid
named.
.SH BUGS
There is no way to kill by program name; a
.I pid
must be looked up first, with
.IR ps (1).
There is no
.IR stop ,
though
.BI /proc/ pid /ctl
takes
.B stop
and
.B start
directly (see
.IR proc (3)).
